n: 9.5.16.24
kind: review
re: 3.11.4.8
saw: 1e20c5f4
ack: node
is: The paper, its date, partial isomorphisms, the priority filter and both stated limits are as the node says. The choice is not ordered both ways: the printer tries the left alternative and then the right, but the parser returns the results of both with unlimited backtracking. The node should say: a constructor one way, a destructor the other; the printer takes alternatives in order, the parser keeps all of them.
why: Read as a PDF in this review, 12 pages. Haskell'10, September 30, 2010 (page 1); a pair of partial functions, one per data constructor, and round trip 'modulo equivalence classes' (3.1); binOpPrio prints parentheses 'exactly where they are needed', example "(7 + 8) * 9" (6.4); 'exponential worst-case complexity and a space leak due to unlimited backtracking' (4.4). Printer choice: 3.3 and 4.2. Parser choice: 4.1 (p s ++ q s); 4.4 warns that a library whose choice commits, such as Parsec's, does not give the semantics syntax descriptions expect.
by: r3/review-host-language-grammars
at: 2026-11-24

n: 9.5.16.25
kind: review
re: 3.11.4.9
saw: d0eca804
ack: 1
is: Re-read through a summarising fetch on 2026-11-24; all three pages say what the node says. The BNFC home page names six target languages (Haskell, Agda, C, C++, Java, OCaml) and lists abstract syntax, pretty-printer, lexer and parser generator files among the outputs.
why: The LBNF reference says comments 'are not passed to the parser', that the pretty-printer 'normalizes' a trailing separator away, and that 'Only the Haskell backends (includes Agda) implement the layout feature'. The Spoofax page says a template's surrounding layout is used to pretty print, and that without it 'we would get the text in a single line separated by spaces'.
by: r3/review-host-language-grammars
at: 2026-11-24

